# State Management (Riverpod)

## Provider conventions
- ✅ Naming:
  - `fooRepositoryProvider`
  - `fooControllerProvider` / `fooNotifierProvider`
- ✅ Providers are declared near feature boundaries (not scattered)
- ✅ Prefer Riverpod primitives that match intent:
  - `AsyncNotifierProvider<*, *>` for stateful async controllers (e.g. login/session)
  - `FutureProvider<T>` for read-only async queries (e.g. backend version)
  - `StateProvider<T>` for simple in-memory state (e.g. `authTokenProvider`)
- ✅ Repository providers should expose *domain interfaces* (e.g. `Provider<AuthRepository>`), not concrete impls

## State shape
- ✅ Prefer explicit state types:
  - `AsyncValue<T>` for async flows
  - sealed/freezed states for complex UI state
- ✅ State is immutable; updates via `copyWith`

## Side effects
- ✅ Side effects live in controllers/notifiers:
  - networking, storage writes, analytics, navigation signals
- ✅ UI reacts to state changes via listeners/effects (not inside build)
- ✅ Prefer `ref.listen(...)` for one-off reactions (snackbars/navigation/router refresh), keeping `ref.watch(...)` for rendering

## Async rules
- ✅ Always handle loading + error states
- ✅ Map exceptions into domain failures (don’t leak Dio/Platform exceptions into UI)
- ✅ Do not store `BuildContext` in notifiers/controllers; pass values in and return state/results

## NEVER
- ❌ Call repositories directly from widgets
- ❌ Store `BuildContext` inside controllers
- ❌ Use global mutable singletons for state
